YOLOv8 stands for You Only Look Once (version 8). It is currently one of the most popular and powerful computer vision models available for object detection.

To understand why it's so widely used—especially for hardware projects like your Reverse Vending Machine—it helps to break down how it works compared to older AI models.

Why is it called "You Only Look Once"?
Before YOLO was invented, older object detection AI used a "two-stage" process.

The AI would scan an image and guess hundreds of different regions where an object might be.

It would then run a heavy classification check on every single one of those regions to see what the object was.

This was accurate, but incredibly slow. It could not process live video feeds effectively.

YOLO revolutionized this by turning the whole process into a single mathematical problem. It passes the image through its neural network exactly once (hence the name). In that single pass, it divides the image into a grid and simultaneously predicts the bounding boxes, the confidence scores, and what the objects are.
